  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 203 AM CST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nStill monitoring some weak radar returns over parts of northeastern \nSouth Dakota early this morning. Webcams and observations indicate \nflurries are ongoing under those returns still. Warm ground \ntemperatures will help limit any additional accumulation, and any \naccumulation is most likely to occur on grassy or elevated surfaces. \nOver the next few hours, expectations are that the lingering snow \nwill continue to push southeastward, and is expected to be out of \nthe region by sunrise. \n\nMeanwhile, over parts of north central South Dakota, fog is \ndeveloping on the back side of the system. High moisture content and \nlight winds create a prime environment for fog to develop. Hi-res \nmodel guidance shows a high degree of confidence in visibility below \nhalf a mile through the morning hours. REFS guidance even gives a 60-\n90% chance for a quarter mile or less over much of central and north \ncentral South Dakota before fog burns over around 15-16Z. As a \nresult, a Dense Fog Advisory has been issued through 16Z to capture \nthe visibility reductions. Areas east of the Missouri River may see \nimproving conditions before 16Z, but have erred on the side of \ncaution for now.\n\nHeight rises in the form of a weak, positively tilted ridge, will \nmove over the region. This will continue the warming trend over the \nnext couple of days. 850mb temperatures by Friday will reach the 5-\n10 degree Celsius range, which is roughly centered around the 90th \npercentile for this time of year. This translates to highs ranging \nfrom the upper 40s to mid 50s, about 10-15 degrees above late-\nNovember normals. \n\nOn Friday, a low pressure system passing to the north will drag a \ncold front across the region. Behind the front, winds will turn \nwesterly and increase. Over the past 24 hour forecast cycle, winds \nhave trended downward in terms of intensity. Along and west of the \nMissouri River are still expected to see the strongest gusts, but \nnow expecting 30-40 miles per hour at peak. Latest NBM probabilities \nto see Advisory strength winds (gusts of 45 miles per hour or \nbetter) has now fallen to 10-20% in that area. The wind is \nstill going to be strong enough to push Grassland Fire Danger up\ninto the High Category, with perhaps some isolated pockets of \nVery High Grassland Fire Danger possible for a couple of hours.",
